'''COLUMN: THE APOLOGY AND THE DELAY — by Wakita Hiroko, Editor-in-Chief'''

The FM spoke. The FM apologised. The FM answered questions. After eleven days of silence, the Foreign Minister appeared before the press corps and took questions for 28 minutes. The apology was direct: "I apologise to the Ayorin community for the delay. The Ministry should have spoken sooner."

The Okami Times accepts the apology. The Okami Times does not accept the explanation — because there was no explanation. "A failure of communication, not a failure of action." But the failure of communication IS the failure of action. The FM did not explain why the delay happened. The FM did not explain why the 90-second statement did not mention the Ayorin attacks. The FM did not explain why a junior spokesperson was sent. The FM said "I should have appeared myself." That is an admission. It is not an explanation.

The Ayorin community responded. Chair Burawa Yuki: "The FM apologised. The apology is accepted. But the FM did not explain why it took eleven days. The apology is a beginning. It is not an ending." The community is right. The apology is a beginning. The questions will continue. The Castellano investigation continues. The Ayorin investigation continues. The diplomatic cascade continues.

The Okami Times notes: the FM spoke for 12 minutes. The FM answered questions for 28 minutes. The FM apologised in one sentence. The community waited eleven days. The ratio is: eleven days of silence, 90 seconds of statement, 12 minutes of opening, 28 minutes of questions, one sentence of apology. The math does not balance. The apology is accepted. The delay is not explained. The questions will continue.

'''THE AKAMATSU GROUP — The Workers Paid. The Executives Stole.'''
The Akamatsu Group is one of Okaiken's largest corporate groups. ¥4.2 trillion in assets. 12,000 employees. Technology, finance, bioengineering. The chairman, the executive VP, and the CFO advisor are in custody. ¥18B embezzled from employee pension funds over three years. Shell companies in Balisca. Falsified financial records. A whistleblower who had the courage to speak.

The Okami Times asks: who is the victim? The 12,000 employees whose pension funds were looted. The workers who paid into the system. The workers who trusted the company. The company betrayed the trust. The executives stole from the workers' retirement. The executives laundered the money through shell companies in Balisca. The executives falsified the financial records for three years. The SESC did not detect the discrepancy. The routine audit did not detect the discrepancy. A mid-level accountant detected the discrepancy. A whistleblower — not the system — exposed the fraud.

The IAA Commission must investigate. The CIRA must enforce concentration limits. The whistleblower must be protected. The workers must be compensated. The Okami Times asks: why did the SESC's routine audit not detect ¥18B in missing pension funds over three years? The system failed. The whistleblower did not. The market reacted. The regulators did not — until the whistleblower spoke. The Akamatsu Group is a cautionary tale of corporate governance failure and regulatory failure. Both failed. The whistleblower did not. The workers paid. The executives stole. The system must answer for both.

